{"type":"output_text","text":{"value":"In the Eclipse JDT (Java Development Tools) AST (Abstract Syntax Tree), the `SwitchCase` class represents a case label in a `switch` statement. With the introduction of Java 12 and the preview feature JEP 325, Java supports "switch rule" syntax using `->` (the arrow), as in:

```java
switch (x) {
    case 1 -> doSomething();
    case 2, 3 -> doAnotherThing();
    default -> doDefault();
}
```

In the Eclipse JDT implementation, the `SwitchCase` node has a property named `SWITCH_LABELED_RULE_PROPERTY`, which is a boolean that indicates whether a particular case uses the "switch rule" syntax (`->`) as opposed to the traditional colon (`:`) syntax. This is also called a "switch labeled rule".

So, for the `CaseStatement` (which in JDT corresponds to `SwitchCase`), the method or property `isSwitchRule` would tell you if the AST node represents a rule-style case (i.e., uses `case ... ->`) instead of the statement group style (`case ... :`). This allows tooling or code analysis to distinguish between the new switch syntax and the old one【3:0†source】.

